#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ef46c96-0ad0-53e2-a066-4466cb3e4014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.45%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361518} {:answer/id #uuid "162ec16a-3b4d-5f4f-94ac-071a40d5c5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.49%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361519} {:answer/id #uuid "976b4899-7903-5cb9-82f0-93e82a108a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.47%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361520} {:answer/id #uuid "d0756de8-e391-5c40-9840-3edde5e9fe77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.55%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361521} {:answer/id #uuid "50506732-0e79-5a81-ae44-6d25598a764a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.52%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "162ec16a-3b4d-5f4f-94ac-071a40d5c5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.49%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361519} :answer-field/id #uuid "b758f262-7cd7-5727-a4fe-c622b22ab767" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a16f5c-99a6-5b86-b6ca-12e8e6087eb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-134529" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(0.9)^{6}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{6}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.9)^{6} & =(1+(-0.1))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.1)+15(-0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1-6(0.1)+15(0.01) \\\\  & =1-0.6+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(0.9)^{6}-0.55|}{(0.9)^{6}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈3.49%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26065fae-e22d-5e29-8331-086f6b5581cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.226" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361525} {:answer/id #uuid "dc07881a-84c7-5e22-bae8-c95a028a9683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.230" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361526} {:answer/id #uuid "70fcc372-cc04-5c53-b750-2145662d52d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.228" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361527} {:answer/id #uuid "04a5e696-215d-52ed-bd0a-c5653ca73a9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361528} {:answer/id #uuid "2483161b-4649-5398-8d85-69ee07e66b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.232" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2483161b-4649-5398-8d85-69ee07e66b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.232" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361529} :answer-field/id #uuid "3904a261-9f3b-50ed-9af7-4be29d6318c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3b60ef2-0e7e-5aa9-a047-53848ed93935" :question/math-academy-id "q-134648" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(3+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(2.96)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =243+5(81)(x)+10(27)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(3+x)}^{5}≈243+405x+270{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.04$ into the above approximation of $(3+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.96)^{5} & =(3+(-0.04))^{5} \\\\  & ≈243+405(-0.04)+270(-0.04)^{2} \\\\  & =243-405(0.04)+270(0.001\\,6) \\\\  & =243-16.2+0.432 \\\\  & =227.232,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c393c44-972e-5df3-8fca-49cbdad70e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890.967" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361532} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ab57bc-ec91-5e7d-8b75-9d62f010dced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890.944" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361533} {:answer/id #uuid "17c99e8d-90d3-5931-b7e5-c94972cd3384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890.825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361534} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3af2ef-0d7c-5251-be0d-2b920e181058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890.734" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361535} {:answer/id #uuid "6bab6ecb-8e9a-5165-bbf1-9228fa0c2a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890.892" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5ab57bc-ec91-5e7d-8b75-9d62f010dced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890.944" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361533} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d24c616-560e-5dd3-b38d-764794e030d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba3987c2-6256-5fcd-86e5-b9ea2e0dfd95" :question/math-academy-id "q-134649" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(3.89)^{5}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{5} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(4)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(4)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(4)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{5}+5{(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\,024+5(256)(x)+10(64)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1\\,024+1\\,280x+640{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(4+x)}^{5}≈1\\,024+1\\,280x+640{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(4+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.89)^{5} & =(4+(-0.11))^{5} \\\\  & ≈1\\,024+1\\,280(-0.11)+640(-0.11)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,024-1\\,280(0.11)+640(0.012\\,1) \\\\  & =1\\,024-140.8+7.744 \\\\  & =890.944,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9cb765-5674-5468-aad6-4e206122bcf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "149.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361539} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2505fb-9eb9-5306-8b96-aceada560c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "152.23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361540} {:answer/id #uuid "6847021e-f1d1-5e7b-b2e4-d1e034ffddad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361541} {:answer/id #uuid "070116c9-ac35-5859-ae1f-1254f662aba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361542} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e179da-26a0-528b-827a-aa7cbf42a47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5e179da-26a0-528b-827a-aa7cbf42a47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361543} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f89c09f-65d9-52bc-86e3-129b69b1c81c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95b99090-20e3-5633-8179-164b3d77b56d" :question/math-academy-id "q-134653" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2+x)^{8}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.87)^{8}.$ Give your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2,$ $b=x,$ and $n=8,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2+x)}^{8} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0}){(2)}^{8}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1}){(2)}^{8-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2}){(2)}^{8-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(2)}^{8}+8{(2)}^{7}{(x)}^{1}+28{(2)}^{6}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =256+8(128)(x)+28(64)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(2+x)}^{8}≈256+1\\,024x+1\\,792{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.13$ into the above approximation of $(2+x)^{8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.87)^{8} & =(2+(-0.13))^{8} \\\\  & ≈256+1\\,024(-0.13)+1\\,792(-0.13)^{2} \\\\  & =256-1\\,024(0.13)+1\\,792(0.016\\,9) \\\\  & =256-133.12+30.284\\,8 \\\\  & =153.16,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87fa59e-ff39-5893-9f73-36a746d12b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.009\\,955" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361546} {:answer/id #uuid "b42493b9-8cc8-527b-852c-dbb0b4ca01b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.999\\,950" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361547} {:answer/id #uuid "30eae97d-bc28-50db-b152-8e61d6d26260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.990\\,043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361548} {:answer/id #uuid "cff75207-ac40-5eb5-aae7-73974a57dcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.990\\,045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361549} {:answer/id #uuid "1370a51f-41f6-5407-bde2-74c0cab3a192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.010\\,045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff75207-ac40-5eb5-aae7-73974a57dcfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.990\\,045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361549} :answer-field/id #uuid "84b4fcd5-82ef-55b9-a533-781c5113666d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b5a6c60-057a-5d0b-a3f1-c5e6e25a1f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-178289" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{10}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.999)^{10}.$ Give your answer to six decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{10} & =1+10x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 9\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{10} \\\\  & =1+10x+45{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{10}≈1+10x+45{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.001$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{10}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.999)^{10} & =(1+(-0.001))^{10} \\\\  & ≈1+10(-0.001)+45(-0.001)^{2} \\\\  & =1-10(0.001)+45(0.000\\,001) \\\\  & =1-0.01+0.000\\,045 \\\\  & =0.990\\,045,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places."}]}
